WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.660
The strongest logging programs begin with
a clear list of security-relevant events.

2
00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:12.514
For agents, that includes prompt and
response metadata,

3
00:00:12.594 --> 00:00:17.765
tool-call metadata, permission checks,
data access events,

4
00:00:17.845 --> 00:00:24.508
high-impact action attempts, policy
denials, user approvals, model version,

5
00:00:24.588 --> 00:00:30.455
prompt version, retrieval source
references, redaction decisions,

6
00:00:30.535 --> 00:00:32.425
and error conditions.

7
00:00:32.505 --> 00:00:39.566
These events help teams understand whether
the agent followed policy and whether it

8
00:00:39.646 --> 00:00:44.021
interacted with sensitive systems
appropriately.

9
00:00:44.101 --> 00:00:47.085
What not to log is just as important.

10
00:00:47.165 --> 00:00:52.237
Secrets, tokens, credentials, unnecessary
personal data,

11
00:00:52.317 --> 00:00:56.891
regulated data, confidential business
information,

12
00:00:56.971 --> 00:01:03.435
and full sensitive documents should not be
captured in plaintext by default.

13
00:01:03.515 --> 00:01:09.383
Logs often have broad operational access
and long retention periods.

14
00:01:09.463 --> 00:01:14.137
If logs become a second copy of everything
the agent saw,

15
00:01:14.217 --> 00:01:18.891
they can become a major privacy and
security liability.

16
00:01:18.971 --> 00:01:25.833
Data minimization means collecting only
what is needed for legitimate security,

17
00:01:25.913 --> 00:01:29.493
operational, audit, or support purposes.

18
00:01:29.573 --> 00:01:34.545
A tool name, resource identifier,
authorization outcome,

19
00:01:34.625 --> 00:01:40.493
and redacted argument summary may be
enough for many investigations.

20
00:01:40.573 --> 00:01:46.440
For some high-risk actions, more detailed
evidence may be justified.

21
00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:51.791
The right balance depends on risk,
regulation, business need,

22
00:01:51.871 --> 00:01:55.352
and the sensitivity of the data involved.

23
00:01:55.432 --> 00:01:59.907
Retention rules should match the purpose
of the logs.

24
00:01:59.987 --> 00:02:03.070
Debug data may have a short lifetime.

25
00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:07.625
Security audit trails may require longer
retention.

26
00:02:07.705 --> 00:02:13.373
Sensitive content should be redacted or
tokenized where possible.

27
00:02:13.453 --> 00:02:17.431
Access to logs should be monitored and
limited.

28
00:02:17.511 --> 00:02:21.489
Good logging does not mean maximum
collection;

29
00:02:21.569 --> 00:02:28.431
it means intentional collection that
supports accountability without creating

30
00:02:28.511 --> 00:02:30.500
unnecessary exposure.
